\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does one fixed finite level of the counting hierarchy decide every existential-universal sentence over the reals encoded by Boolean combinations of sign tests on integer arithmetic-circuit polynomials, with signed binary constants and only addition, subtraction and multiplication gates, independent of variable counts, degrees and coefficient magnitudes?

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does one fixed finite level of the counting hierarchy decide every existential-universal sentence over the reals encoded by Boolean combinations of sign tests on integer arithmetic-circuit polynomials, with signed binary constants and only addition, subtraction and multiplication gates, independent of variable counts, degrees and coefficient magnitudes?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18006}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

